<p><strong>A compromised gateway can speak for its whole attestor lane.</strong> Restrict the namespace grant, then narrow conversation scope in the Cedar policies that grant capabilities. The local development transport accepts a caller-declared supported subject; it does not independently authenticate the sender.</p>
<h3>Chart-aligned mapping excerpt</h3>
<p>This is part of <code>broker.yaml</code>, not a complete configuration. An attestor without <code>namespaces</code> can attest any mapped subject; the one below limits it to one Slack workspace. Use the actual gateway UID on a native host, rather than copying the chart's UID.</p>
<pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Illustrative broker identity excerpt"><code>identities:
  - uid: 65533
    principal: dekopon-gatewayd
    actor: { type: service, principal: dekopon-gatewayd }
    attestor:
      namespaces: [slack.t0123abcd]
principals:
  alice:
    subjects: [slack.t0123abcd.u0123abcd]</code>
</pre>
<p>Keep the gateway peer principal named <code>dekopon-gatewayd</code>: the broker requires that name and an attestor grant for the gateway's startup/reload handshake. A mapped peer without an attestation receives no capability. The broker's own-UID health probe proves reachability, not permission for a chat sender.</p>
</section>
<section id="policy">
<h2>Alice needs two grants.</h2>
<p>Permission to prompt <code>pr-commenter</code> does not permit a GitHub write. A separate rule permits the comment capability only through that agent and gateway. This illustrative publishing example is separate from the read-only reviewer above. It uses a direct-message session; a mapped channel also needs the channel's grants below.</p>
<pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Illustrative Cedar policy excerpt"><code>@id("alice-prompt")
permit(principal == Dekopon::Principal::"alice",
       action == Dekopon::Action::"agent.prompt",
       resource == Dekopon::Agent::"pr-commenter")
when { context has via &amp;&amp; context.via == "dekopon-gatewayd" };

@id("alice-comment")
permit(principal == Dekopon::Principal::"alice",
       action == Dekopon::Action::"gh.pull-request.comment",
       resource == Dekopon::Provider::"gh")
when { context has agent &amp;&amp; context.agent == "pr-commenter"
    &amp;&amp; context has via &amp;&amp; context.via == "dekopon-gatewayd" };</code>
</pre>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Request comparison">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Request</th>
<th scope="col">Expected decision with these rules</th>
<th scope="col">Why</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Alice prompts pr-commenter via dekopon-gatewayd</th>
<td data-label="Expected decision with these rules">Allow</td>
<td data-label="Why">Matches alice-prompt</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Alice comments through pr-commenter</th>
<td data-label="Expected decision with these rules">Allow, if the capability is loaded and constrained</td>
<td data-label="Why">Matches alice-comment; HTTP still has separate checks</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Alice requests merge</th>
<td data-label="Expected decision with these rules">Deny</td>
<td data-label="Why">No matching permit; no merge invocation</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>These are worked decisions, not a deployed test. Cedar evaluates static rules, not intent; a permitted comment can still contain harmful text. A model can help draft configuration, but runtime decisions use the saved files.</p>
<p>The provider resource is <code>gh</code>, not an individual repository. This rule does not inspect arbitrary provider input. Restrict the upstream GitHub token's repository access; a fine-grained PAT limited to selected private repositories still permits public-repository reads. Likewise, <code>allowedHosts: [api.github.com]</code> constrains the destination host, not repository names.</p>
</section>
<section id="spaces">
<h2>A mapped channel narrows every participant's access.</h2>
<p>A Slack channel subject such as <code>slack.t0123abcd.c0123abcd</code>, or a Discord channel subject such as <code>discord.123456789.987654321</code>, can map to a separate principal under <code>principals</code>. The broker derives this space from the authenticated conversation; the model cannot select it.</p>
<p>The reviewer recipe also restricts the gateway route to one workspace and channel. Use lowercase canonical IDs in <code>conversation</code>; the channel ID stays in <code>ids</code> for thread messages too. Replace these placeholders with the IDs used in the broker mapping above:</p>
<pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Exact Slack channel route excerpt"><code>conversation:
  kind: [channel, thread]
  container: t0123abc
  ids: [c0123abc]</code></pre>
<p>In that channel and its threads, both the sender and the space must allow the agent prompt and each capability, secret use and HTTP decision. A participant's wider personal grant cannot override a restrictive channel. You may leave a channel unmapped, retaining the sender's existing grants. A mapped channel with no permits allows nothing. Direct messages and top-level group DMs have no space principal.</p>
<p>Review private repositories in a private Slack channel whose members may see the code and results. Grants control who can invoke the agent; they don't hide its replies from other channel members. The selected model receives reviewed content. The recipe keeps history private per subject; opting into shared conversation history also lets participants' retained input influence later turns, including an owner's permitted write.</p>
<p>GitHub webhook sessions instead use a repository principal and need their own prompt and capability grants. Signed event facts do not prove the current PR head or checks: the agent must read those through permitted tools. Cedar evaluates author-set facts both absent and present, so those facts can restrict access but cannot create permission.</p>
</section>
<section id="constraints">
<h2>A Cedar allow does not widen HTTP access.</h2>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Owner-controlled constraint comparison">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Owner-controlled constraint</th>
<th scope="col">Enforced independently of policy</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Destination + method</th>
<td data-label="Enforced independently of policy">For the comment example: api.github.com and GET/POST, not arbitrary hosts or methods.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Call / byte / time ceilings</th>
<td data-label="Enforced independently of policy">The provider may need a pre-read plus a write; every call consumes the invocation budget. Host ceilings still cap configured values.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Loaded components</th>
<td data-label="Enforced independently of policy">Use strict: true to refuse startup on policy actions whose providers are not loaded. Default non-strict mode warns; absent capabilities still cannot execute.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Catalog descriptions tell the model what an agent is for; they do not authorize it. The <a href="{{ source }}/crates/dekopon-brokerd/README.md#configuration">pinned broker contract ↗</a> defines the current fields. Install a compatible provider and validate the configuration against its loaded manifest; copying a policy alone does not install its GitHub component. The <a href="/deploy/mac/">Mac path</a> starts with the read-only GitHub reviewer.</p>
</section>
<section id="credentials">
<h2>The native HTTP sink sends the credential.</h2>
<p>Existing named credential bindings, optionally selected per agent, stay in broker configuration. The guest builds its request without receiving the value; the native HTTP host injects it for the bound destination. That endpoint receives the credential.</p>
<p>For a GitHub PAT on a Mac, <code>credentialsPath</code> points to a private broker-owned <code>0600</code> YAML file. Its <code>bearerToken</code> entry contains the literal PAT, bound to <code>api.github.com</code>; the configured capability selects that entry by name. launchd doesn't source <code>.env</code>, and the broker doesn't expand <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>. Keep this PAT out of the gateway's environment. The <a href="{{ site.repository }}/blob/main/docs/run-on-mac.md#github-pat-under-launchd">PAT and launchd procedure ↗</a> supplies the exact file and service steps.</p>
<p>A public DRN is a logical secret name, not a credential value. A provider command can propose one, or the owner can configure a capability's <code>defaultSecretUse</code> bearer binding. Either route still needs the exact DRN's <code>secret.use</code> permission and a private binding for the sink, method and path/query scope. A DRN pasted into ordinary provider input is just text. The broker pins each resolved value to its invocation and never serves a stale fallback.</p>
<p>Chat replies and model requests use gateway-held credentials directly. Broker policy governs provider effects; it does not govern every API call the gateway's chat token can make.</p>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Refreshing secret sources">
<table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Private source</th><th scope="col">What the broker does</th><th scope="col">Operator responsibility</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row"><code>kubernetesTokenRequest</code></th><td data-label="What the broker does">Mints a token once per authorized invocation for a fixed namespace, ServiceAccount and audience. No cached token or stale fallback.</td><td data-label="Operator responsibility">Mount a live API-audience bootstrap token and CA only in the broker. Restrict <code>serviceaccounts/token</code> RBAC to the target account; configure the destination’s issuer, audience, subject and expiry checks.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><code>oauth2Refresh</code></th><td data-label="What the broker does">Locks and rereads a private version-2 record, refreshes near expiry, and atomically saves a rotated token. Optional scheduled refresh also runs while idle; the guest receives neither token.</td><td data-label="Operator responsibility">Use a regular single-link <code>0600</code> record in a writable persistent directory. A retired family needs a new private seed or reauthorization; follow the source's reset procedure.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>These private-map sources still need both the capability grant and the exact DRN’s <code>secret.use</code> grant. They do not replace the separate legacy ChatGPT credential binding or its account header; that migration has not shipped.</p>
<p><strong>Pick endpoints you trust.</strong> Raw-secret response checks cannot stop an authorized endpoint from encoding or transforming the credential it received. A secret binding restricts where that secret goes; it does not narrow every credential-free request the capability can make.</p>
